WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.437 --> 00:00:03.773
One database query can wreck a growing fintech app.

2
00:00:03.988 --> 00:00:06.335
The problem might not even be traffic.

3
00:00:06.804 --> 00:00:07.865
Meet PAYNOVA.

4
00:00:08.105 --> 00:00:11.277
A fast-growing fintech startup processing transactions,

5
00:00:11.604 --> 00:00:15.689
balances, and transaction histories for thousands of active customers.

6
00:00:16.223 --> 00:00:19.482
But one history query kept pulling far too much data.

7
00:00:19.722 --> 00:00:21.807
Instead of an efficient access path,

8
00:00:21.935 --> 00:00:25.640
the database repeatedly scanned the entire transactions table for one

9
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:26.858
user's records.

10
00:00:27.378 --> 00:00:31.738
As traffic increased, that inefficient query consumed more resources.

11
00:00:31.978 --> 00:00:34.413
Requests began piling up in queues.

12
00:00:34.628 --> 00:00:38.376
The API slowed down, and the whole application felt the pressure.

13
00:00:38.869 --> 00:00:42.717
Daniel opened the execution plan and found the real bottleneck.

14
00:00:42.957 --> 00:00:46.050
The database was performing a full table scan instead of

15
00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.792
an efficient indexed lookup on the right columns.

16
00:00:49.293 --> 00:00:52.548
The team optimized the query and added an appropriate composite

17
00:00:52.668 --> 00:00:54.554
index based on the query pattern,

18
00:00:54.894 --> 00:00:56.791
selectivity, and sort order.

19
00:00:57.018 --> 00:01:00.666
The new plan retrieved the right records far more efficiently.

20
00:01:01.132 --> 00:01:02.855
The application recovered.

21
00:01:03.083 --> 00:01:04.942
But the real lesson was bigger.

22
00:01:05.182 --> 00:01:07.618
Database performance has to be monitored,

23
00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:12.680
tested, and designed before rapid growth exposes the hidden weaknesses.

24
00:01:13.189 --> 00:01:16.069
What would you investigate first: the database,

25
00:01:16.189 --> 00:01:17.737
the API, or the queue?
